@bors r-

We discussed this extensively in the last @rust-lang/libs-api meeting, and many people had concerns about tying this to overflow checks. In particular, people may want to have overflow checks enabled (for arithmetic operations) but still not want `Instant` operations to panic (because that will break for reasons outside their control, such as running on buggy VMs/hardware where time appears to go backwards). And we don't want to discourage people from enabling overflow checks at all. The key argument from the meeting: "if overflow checks were free, we'd always want them enabled, but we don't always want *this* enabled".

We subsequently did an informal poll on Zulip. The question of which alternative people would *prefer* had a broader spectrum of preferences. However, the question of which alternatives people *object to* was quite definitive. There was only one option that nobody on the team objected to: Always saturate and return 0 if the "later" time is earlier than the "earlier" time.

Given that we've changed out minds before, I don't want to ask @the8472 to proceed down that path without confirming that consensus.

So, can we establish consensus on the approach of always saturating in `duration_since`, `sub`, and `elapsed`?

