@JohnTitor 
Could you add a test case not involving `let else` like suggested in https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/pull/89965#issuecomment-945070808?
If that's not possible, then we can close this in favor of https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/pull/89841.

Had a thought as I was writing the explainer:

We might need to extend these errors. Consider this case:

```rust
trait Iterable {
    type Item<'a>;

    type Iterator<'a>: Iterator<Item = Self::Item<'a>>;

    fn iter<'a>(&'a self) -> Self::Iterator<'a>;
}
```

Here, the method implies that `Iterator` needs `where Self: 'a`, but `Item` does too. I think we can see that from the fact that references to `Item<'a>` appear in the bounds of `Iterator`, and we inferred that `'a` represents the "lifetime of a borrow from Self" when using `Iterator`, so it probably does for `Item` too.

❤️ keep 'em coming!

