@calebcartwright I might be missing something here but I don't think this change introduces a lot of complexity to rustfmt. This is what I imagine would happen:

### If user wrote a top-level type alias with where after `=`..

```rust
type Foo<T> = u32 where T: Debug;
```

This would be an error today. It will eventually be made to work, but not until we figure out how to give it the semantics we expect. It will parse, however, so rustfmt *could* rewrite it to the obvious formatting:

```rust
type Foo<T> = u32
where
    T: Debug;
```

### If user wrote a top-level type alias with where before `=`..

```rust
type Foo<T> where T: Debug = u32;
```

No change. Do whatever rustfmt does now. This may eventually be deprecated, but it won't be removed; it would not make sense for rustfmt to rewrite it because there would be a semantic shift involved (as you noted). Most likely, it would mean the where clauses go from not being enforced to being enforced.

### If user wrote a generic associated type with where after `=`...

```rust
impl ... {
    type Foo<T> = u32 where T: Debug;
}
```

This would be formatted like so, I expect. This seems like the obvious way to do things, but let me know if you think we should write a separate rustfmt RFC:

```rust
impl ... {
    type Foo<T> = u32
    where
        T: Debug;
}
```

### If user wrote a generic associated type with where before `=`...

This would be a compilation error. The compiler would suggest a rewrite. rustfmt would not necessarily be expected to do anything here, although I think it would be **useful** if rustfmt would do the rewrite on the user's behalf. I expect that the parser will parse this result, but it would fail ast validation, so that seems within scope for rustfmt. Note that, unlike in the case of free-standing type aliases, this is not a "semantics changing operation". We are going from "compilation error" to "successful compile":

```rust
impl ... {
    type Foo<T> where T: Debug = u32;
}
```

becomes

```rust
impl ... {
    type Foo<T> = u32
    where
        T: Debug;
}
```

